    "body": "> Reviewed commit: `21fc1ca1d2fd049e82e111c1046b443f0cd98697`\n\nThis PR successfully optimizes the Timer and HR Tiles for vertical space density, delivering on its stated goal. The changes significantly improve the compactness and visual hierarchy of these critical dashboard components.\n\n### Key Improvements & Observations\n\n1.  **Vertical Space Optimization**: Both `HrTile` and `TimerDisplay` have been refactored to use vertical space much more efficiently. This is evident in the reduced heights for `SpotifyControls` and `Skeleton` components, as well as the more compact internal layouts of the tiles themselves.\n2.  **Code Conciseness (`HrTile.tsx`)**: The refactoring of `HrTile` is particularly noteworthy. By integrating the logic from `IdentityTier`, `HeroTier`, `MetricItem`, and `DataTier` directly into the main `HrTile` component, approximately 90 lines of code were removed. This significantly reduces complexity and adheres to the \"Code Conciseness & Redundancy Prevention\" guideline from `.github/copilot-instructions.md`.\n3.  **Enhanced `TimerDisplay`**: The introduction of `CircularProgress` as a visual indicator for timer progress is a great addition, enhancing the user experience without adding visual clutter. The responsive adjustments for mobile (`isMobile`) also improve adaptability.\n4.  **MUI & Theming Adherence**: The use of `alpha` for color manipulation in `HrTile` and the consistent application of MUI components and `sx` props align perfectly with the `DESIGN_GUIDELINES.md` and `.github/copilot-instructions.md`.\n5.  **Path Alias Correction**: The import path for `TimerControls` was correctly updated to use the `@/` alias, which is a good practice as per `.github/copilot-instructions.md`.\n6.  **Responsive Layout (`HrmConnectionPanel.tsx`)**: The switch from flexbox to CSS Grid for `HrmConnectionPanel` provides a more robust and flexible responsive layout for the HR tiles.\n7.  **VRT Updates**: The necessary updates to the Playwright VRT snapshots and the mask selector for `hr-tile-card` indicate thoroughness in adapting tests to the UI changes.\n\nOverall, this is a well-executed PR that delivers significant frontend improvements and reduces technical debt through code simplification.\n\n> Reviewed at commit: `21fc1ca1d2fd049e82e111c1046b443f0cd98697`\n",
